<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Colossus of Rhodes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 280–226 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Colossus of Helios</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Colossus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> was a bronze statue of the sun god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helios">Helios</a>, about 33 metres high, made by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chares-of-lindos">Chares of Lindos</a> to celebrate the city's survival of the siege by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-i-of-macedon">Demetrius Poliorcetes</a> in 305–304 BCE. Completed around 280 BCE, it was one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-wonders-of-the-ancient-world">Seven Wonders of the Ancient World</a>. An earthquake toppled it around 226 BCE, and its remains lay where they fell for centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colossus-of-rhodes.jpg" alt="Colossus of Rhodes" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: gravure sur bois de Sidney Barclay numérisée Google · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colosse_de_Rhodes_(Barclay).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the late fourth century BCE Rhodes, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, resisted an invasion by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus I Monophthalmus</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-i-of-macedon">Demetrius I of Macedon</a> besieged the city for a year, and in 304 BCE a relief force sent by Ptolemy arrived and he abandoned the siege, leaving most of his equipment. The Rhodians sold it for 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> and decided to build a colossal statue of their patron god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helios">Helios</a>. Chares of Lindos directed the work, which began in 292 BCE. His teacher Lysippos had made a 22-metre bronze statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Most descriptions give the statue a height of about 70 cubits. It stood on a white marble pedestal near the harbour entrance, though other sources place it on a breakwater. Accounts describe an iron frame with bronze plates as a skin, filled with stone blocks as work proceeded. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philo-of-byzantium">Philo of Byzantium</a> wrote that Chares cast it in horizontal courses, burying each section in earth. The image of the statue straddling the harbour mouth is a medieval invention, and engineering analysis indicates that a bronze statue with legs apart would have collapsed. Its true location remains in dispute.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The statue stood for 54 years. An earthquake in 226 BCE snapped it at the knees. Ptolemy III offered to pay for rebuilding, but the Oracle of Delphi led the Rhodians to fear they had offended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helios">Helios</a>, and they declined. The remains lay for over 800 years and drew visitors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> called it the best of the city's votive offerings, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> wrote that few men could clasp its thumb in their arms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophanes-the-confessor">Theophanes the Confessor</a> records that in 653 an Arab force sold its bronze to a Jewish merchant, who loaded it on 900 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>; he is the only source, and L. I. Conrad considers the story likely propaganda.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Colossus of Rhodes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Colossus of Rhodes</b> is said to have been paid for by selling the siege engines that Demetrius Poliorcetes left behind after his failed siege of the city in 305–304 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Colossus</b> fell in an earthquake, its remains lay where they had fallen for centuries; Pliny the Elder wrote that few people could wrap their arms around its thumb.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
